Chapter: Truck
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Rider
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils

 The truck the rider is chasing: how far it has driven along the route, its speed,
 and whether it is slowing this frame.

 A partial port of wasm/truck.zig -- THE MOTION HALF ONLY, and the split is the
 zig's own. `State`, `initial`, `nextTurn` and `next` reach nothing but the world
 and a few of the rider's constants. The drawing half -- the trailer box, its
 tires, the headlight wedges -- reaches `render.Chain`, `render.Pose` and
 `render.at`, and it is `Safari chapter TruckBody`, which cites Render for exactly
 that. The split is what keeps World's cite of this chapter cheap: placing a truck
 costs five constants, and only the chapters that DRAW one pay for the mapping
 spine. Cat and CatDraw are split for the same reason.

 THREE RULES AND NOTHING ELSE: brake before a turn, accelerate when behind
 schedule, cruise when ahead. The schedule is a lerp -- it starts the truck five
 hundred metres ahead and closes that to a hundred by the course end, which is a
 photo finish rather than a catch.

 Every one of its margins is expressed against the rider's own constants rather
 than repeated: it accelerates ten percent harder, tops out ten percent faster,
 brakes over the same distance, and takes each corner at four fifths of the
 rider's safe speed. So a change to the rider's physics moves the truck with it.

 We say:

Section: The Chase Schedule

  start-ahead : Real
  start-ahead = 500.0

 The lead the schedule closes DOWN to by the course end -- not zero, because the
 point is a photo finish.

  finish-lead : Real
  finish-lead = 100.0

  truck-turn-caution : Real
  truck-turn-caution = 0.8

  truck-brake-distance : Real
  truck-brake-distance = approach-intersection-dist

  truck-chase-accel : Real
  truck-chase-accel = 1.1 * a-accel

  truck-max-v : Real
  truck-max-v = 1.1 * v-max

Section: State

  TruckState = record {
    pos : Real,
    v : Real,
    braking : Boolean
  }

 At the start: a long way down the road, idling at the rider's base speed, not
 braking.

  truck-initial : TruckState
  truck-initial = TruckState { pos = start-ahead, v = v-base, braking = False }

Section: The Next Turn

 How far to the next real TURN ahead of `pos`, and the rider's safe speed for it.
 Past the course end there is no turn to brake for and the truck cruises in.

 The zig answers infinity for "no turn", which Codex has no literal for. A sentinel
 would be worse than saying what is meant, so the absence is carried in the TYPE:
 `Maybe Turn` from `Foreword chapter Maybe`.

 IT USED TO BE A `found` BOOLEAN BESIDE THE PAYLOAD, which is a Maybe with the tag
 unhooked from the thing it guards -- nothing stops a caller reading `dist` without
 testing `found`, and nothing but prose says the two agree. `when` forces both arms
 and there is no unguarded field to read. The one caller reads better for it too.

  Turn = record {
    dist : Real,
    v-turn : Real
  }

  next-turn-loop : List Segment, Real, Integer, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn-loop (segs) (pos) (i) (cum0) =
    if i >= list-length segs then None
    else next-turn-step segs pos i (cum0 + (list-at segs i).length)

  next-turn-step : List Segment, Real, Integer, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn-step (segs) (pos) (i) (cum) =
    let s = list-at segs i
    in if pos < cum
      then (if s.terminates then None else Just (Turn { dist = cum - pos, v-turn = turn-speed s.exit-angle }))
      else next-turn-loop segs pos (i + 1) cum

  next-turn : List Segment, Real -> Maybe Turn
  next-turn (segs) (pos) = next-turn-loop segs pos 0 0.0

Section: One Frame

 Advance the truck one RIDER frame. `rider-dist` is how far the rider has now
 driven, and `l` is the course length.

 The kinematic brake arrives at the turn at the caution speed and is recomputed
 every frame, so it tracks rather than committing. The brake lights are lit only
 while it is ACTUALLY slowing -- the flag is a comparison against the previous
 speed, not against the intent.

 WHERE THE TRUCK "SHOULD" BE, given how far the rider has come: the lerp the
 whole chase hangs on, named rather than left inside `truck-next` because the
 browser's SCRUB needs it too. A scrubbed still has no state to step, so it places
 the truck on its schedule and accepts that the schedule is where the truck is
 AIMING rather than where three rules would have put it -- the same stand-in, and
 the same honesty about it, that the scrubbed rider gets.

  truck-schedule : Real, Real -> Real
  truck-schedule (rider-dist) (l) =
    rider-dist + finish-lead + (start-ahead - finish-lead) * (1.0 - rider-dist / l)

  truck-next : TruckState, Real, List Segment, Real -> TruckState
  truck-next (truck) (rider-dist) (segs) (l) =
    let scheduled = truck-schedule rider-dist l
    in when next-turn segs truck.pos
       is Just (turn) -> (if turn.dist <= truck-brake-distance then truck-braking truck turn else truck-free truck scheduled)
       is None -> truck-free truck scheduled

  truck-braking : TruckState, Turn -> TruckState
  truck-braking (truck) (turn) =
    let target = turn.v-turn * truck-turn-caution
    in let a = if turn.dist > 0.000001 then (target * target - truck.v * truck.v) / (2.0 * turn.dist) else 0.0
    in let v = real-max target (truck.v + a)
    in TruckState { pos = truck.pos + v, v = v, braking = v < truck.v }

 Behind schedule it accelerates, capped; ahead of schedule and not braking, it
 holds its speed.

  truck-free : TruckState, Real -> TruckState
  truck-free (truck) (scheduled) =
    let v = if truck.pos < scheduled then real-min truck-max-v (truck.v + truck-chase-accel) else truck.v
    in TruckState { pos = truck.pos + v, v = v, braking = False }
